  "updated": "2026-08-20T07:09:10.041309+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light southerly flow has redeveloped areawide \nthis morning, and dewpoints are starting out in the mid to upper\n70s which is higher than the low to mid 70s yesterday morning. \nMeanwhile, ensemble and EFI guidance point towards another day \nof above normal highs. This combination should support a larger \nfootprint of heat indices in excess of 105 degrees compared to \nyesterday. As such, the Heat Advisory covers a larger area \ntoday. Additionally, the advisory has been expanded further \nnorth in the coastal plain, and now includes Greene, Pitt, and \nBeaufort Counties. Heat indices may once again flirt with 105 \ndegrees on Friday. However, increased cloudcover and an \nincreased risk of thunderstorms should temper this risk some. A \nsmaller Heat Advisory may be needed on Friday, especially if \nthunderstorm activity is delayed later into the day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Light southerly flow has help boost dewpoints \ninto the mid to upper 70s this morning. Even with some mixing \nthis afternoon, heating of the more moist boundary layer should \nsupport strong instability developing by this afternoon with \nMLCAPE forecast to reach 2000-3000j/kg. Deep layer shear will be\nweak (10-20kt), favoring poorly organized multicells. During \nthe day, the primary forcing mechanisms look to be the seabreeze\nand an inland trough. Convergence doesn't look great with \neither, and this may tend to keep convective coverage isolated \nto widely scattered during the daylight hours. The primary risk \nwith the strongest cells should be gusty outflow winds of 40-50 \nmph. \n\nLater this evening (ie. after 10pm), a broken line of \nthunderstorms is forecast to be approaching the area from the \nNW. This convection will be tied to a cold front that will be \nmoving SE out of Virginia. Most short-term guidance insist that \nthis convection will be on a weakening trend as it approaches \nENC. However, given the increased forcing along the front, the \nconvection may last longer than some guidance suggests, even if \nelevated/weak in nature. Prior to more substantial weakening, a \nrisk of strong to marginally severe outflow winds may accompany \nthe thunderstorms as they reach the far northern reaches of ENC.\n\n\nOn Friday, the cold front is forecast to stall across the \ncentral and eastern Carolinas, and will interact with a very \nmoist and strongly unstable airmass, favoring the development of\nnumerous thunderstorms. Deep layer shear should get a bump up \nin the vicinity of the front, and synoptically this appears to \nbe a decent setup for several instances of severe weather. The \nprimary risk should once again be strong to damaging wind gusts.\nHowever, given the slightly better shear and a signal for \nrotating updrafts, a hail risk may develop in some of the \nstrongest/tallest cores. \n\nIn addition to the severe weather potential, there will also be\na risk of intense rainfall rates and hydro concerns. Strong \ninstability, 2\"+ PWATs, increased large-scale and mesoscale \nforcing, and the potential for training convection all point \ntowards a risk of flooding, including at least a few instances \nof flash flooding. It's possible that a Flood Watch may be \nneeded for a portion of the area. The potential for scattered to\nnumerous thunderstorms looks to continue into Saturday. \nHowever, instability will be lower, and this may lead to a \nlowered risk of severe weather. Heavy rain and flooding concerns\nmay continue, though.",
